<td>14.8</td><td>14.0</td><td>1.7</td><td>1.4</td></tr><tr><td>Fifth War Loan</td><td>72,113</td><td>204</td><td>49,530</td><td>11,281</td><td>9,241</td><td>1,075</td><td>782</td><td>100.0</td><td>0.3</td><td>68.7</td><td>15.6</td><td>12.8</td><td>1.5</td><td>1.1</td></tr><tr><td>Sixth War Loan</td><td>66,132</td><td>1,488</td><td>43,560</td><td>10,226</td><td>9,007</td><td>1,085</td><td>766</td><td>100.0</td><td>2.2</td><td>65.9</td><td>15.5</td><td>13.6</td><td>1.6</td><td>1.2</td></tr></table>\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"table\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [91, 1269, 574, 1311], \"page_id\": 25}], \"content\": \"The $10 denomination Series E bond was authorized by the Secretary of the Treasury on June 7, 1944, and was made available only to members of the Military and Naval Forces\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 12, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [639, 1269, 1068, 1310], \"page_id\": 25}], \"content\": \"of the United States through such agencies as might be provided within their respective establishments by the Secretary of War and the Secretary of the Navy.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 13, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [550, 39, 594, 56], \"page_id\": 26}], \"content\": \"A-18\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [907, 31, 1081, 58], \"page_id\": 26}], \"content\": \"Treasury Bulletin\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [91, 103, 1046, 154], \"page_id\": 26}], \"content\": \"Security Purchases Outside the Sixth War Loan by Commercial Banks and Treasury Investment Accounts\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"title\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [67, 200, 261, 215], \"page_id\": 26}], \"content\": \"Commercial Banks\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [67, 221, 540, 548], \"page_id\": 26}], \"content\": \"Concurrently with the Sixth War Loan, commercial banks were permitted to subscribe in limited amounts to the 2% and 2-1/2% Treasury bonds offered in the drive and to Series F and G savings bonds. The limit on such purchases was set at 10% of the combined amount of time certificates of deposit of individuals and nonprofit corporations or associations, and of savings deposits, but not more than $500,000 for any one bank. Purchases of the Series F and G savings bonds were restricted further by the regular annual limit of $100,000 issue price per subscriber for both issues combined. Subscriptions by commercial banks for the limited investment of time deposits were not considered as part of the Sixth War Loan nor were they credited toward attainment of the quotas.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [66, 567, 541, 643], \"page_id\": 26}], \"content\": \"Subscriptions by commercial banks to the issues available to them under these limits aggregated $1,014 millions. Distribution of the purchases by issues is presented below.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [596, 185, 1072, 370], \"page_id\": 26}], \"content\": \"<table><tr><th>Description of security</th><th>Amount purchased (In millions of dollars)</th></tr><tr><td>2% Treasury bonds, 12/15/52-54....</td><td>886</td></tr><tr><td>2-1/2% Treasury bonds, 3/15/66-71...</td><td>72</td></tr><tr><td>Series F savings bonds....................</td><td>19</td></tr><tr><td>Series G savings bonds....................</td><td>37</td></tr><tr><td>Total....................</td><td>1,014</td></tr></table>\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"table\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [598, 409, 935, 428], \"page_id\": 26}], \"content\": \"Treasury Investment Accounts\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [596, 436, 1067, 645], \"page_id\": 26}], \"content\": \"Subscriptions by Treasury investment accounts totaled $762 millions during the period of the Sixth War Loan. These were divided between $662 millions of the 2-1/2% and $100 millions of the 2% Treasury bonds offered in the drive. These purchases, however, like those permitted to commercial banks, were not attributed to sales in the Sixth War Loan. Treasury investment accounts are those United States Government agencies and trust funds whose investments are handled through the facilities of the Treasury Department.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [91, 740, 1031, 787], \"page_id\": 26}], \"content\": \"Treasury Market Financing: Refunding of December 1 Certificates and 4% Treasury Bonds\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [65, 801, 544, 919], \"page_id\": 26}], \"content\": \"Exchange offerings were made concurrently with the Sixth War Loan for two outstanding Treasury securities. These constituted the only market financing operations, other than the regular weekly offerings of Treasury bills, in November and December 1944.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [66, 938, 541, 1145], \"page_id\": 26}], \"content\": \"On November 20, the day on which the Sixth War Loan drive commenced, subscription books were opened for the exchange of the 7\\u00bd% certificates of indebtedness due December 1, 1944, into O.90% Treasury notes dated December 1, 1944, and maturing January 1, 1946. Subscription books on this offering were closed on November 22, except that subscriptions amounting to $100,000 or less were accepted through November 25. Of the $3,540 millions of maturing certificates, $3,416 1/2 millions, or 96% were exchanged.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [66, 1162, 540, 1469], \"page_id\": 26}], \"content\": \"On November 20, the Treasury also offered holders of the 4% Treasury bonds of 1944-54, outstanding in the amount of $1,037 millions, and called for redemption on December 15, 1944, an opportunity to exchange their holdings into three of the securities offered in the Sixth War Loan, namely the 1-1/4% Treasury notes due September 15, 1947, the 2% Treasury bonds of December 15, 1952-54, and the 2-1/2% Treasury bonds of March 15, 1966-71, subject to the proviso that the 2-1/2% bonds may not be held by commercial banks for their own account prior to December 1, 1954. Subscription books for this exchange transaction remained open through December 9, except for subscriptions of $100,000 or less, which were received up to and including December 15, the\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 12, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [594, 806, 1070, 936], \"page_id\": 26}], \"content\": \"payment date for the called bonds. Exchanges amounted to $907 millions, or 87% of the outstanding amount of the 4% bonds. These subscriptions were not counted toward the goals in the Sixth War Loan. The distribution of accepted exchanges among the three new securities is shown in the following table:\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 13, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [594, 950, 1068, 1218], \"page_id\": 26}], \"content\": \"<table><tr><th>New issue</th><th>Amount<br/>(In millions of dollars)</th><th>Percent of total</th></tr><tr><td>1-1/4% Treasury note, 9/15/47</td><td>137</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>2% Treasury bond, 12/15/52-54</td><td>737</td><td>81</td></tr><tr><td>2-1/2% Treasury bond, 3/15/66-71</td><td>33</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>Total exchanges of 4% Treasury bonds of 1944-54</td><td>907</td><td>100</td></tr></table>\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 14, \"type\": \"table\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [594, 1227, 1067, 1396], \"page_id\": 26}], \"content\": \"The weekly offering of Treasury bills was reduced from $1,300 millions to $1,200 millions or thereabouts, with the issue dated November 30. For seven previous weeks the weekly offering had been $1,300 millions of which $1,200 millions was offset by weekly maturities of bills and $100 millions represented new money. The December weekly offering rate of $1,200 millions was equal to weekly maturities.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 15, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [593, 1418, 1062, 1467], \"page_id\": 26}], \"content\": \"1/ This figure has been revised from the preliminary figure reported in the table on page 32 of this issue of the \\\"Treasury Bulletin\\\".\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 16, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [74, 14, 218, 42], \"page_id\": 27}], \"content\": \"January 1945\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [568, 22, 582, 41], \"page_id\": 27}], \"content\": \"1\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [253, 703, 895, 729], \"page_id\": 27}], \"content\": \"SUMMARY OF FISCAL STATISTICS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [559, 32, 576, 51], \"page_id\": 28}], \"content\": \"2\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [902, 24, 1080, 53], \"page_id\": 28}], \"content\": \"Treasury Bulletin\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [389, 87, 745, 103], \"page_id\": 28}], \"content\": \"Budgetary Receipts and Expenditures\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [479, 122, 643, 136], \"page_id\": 28}], \"content\": \"(In millions of dollars)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [44, 139, 1086, 1062], \"page_id\": 28}], \"content\": \"<table><tr><th rowspan=\\\"2\\\"></th><th colspan=\\\"4\\\">First 5 months, fiscal years 1943 to 1945</th><th colspan=\\\"7\\\">Complete fiscal years, 1941 to 1945</th></tr><tr><th>1943</th><th>1944</th><th>1945</th><th>Change 1944 to 1945</th><th colspan=\\\"3\\\">Actual</th><t